<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deng Xiaoping</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1904">1904</a>–<a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deng Xiaoping was a Chinese communist revolutionary who was the paramount leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> from 1978 until the early 1990s. Purged twice during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, he returned after Mao's death and launched the policy of reform and opening, restoring family farming, creating Special Economic Zones and admitting foreign investment. He also approved the armed suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1989-tiananmen-square-protests-and-massacre">Tiananmen Square protests</a> in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deng-xiaoping.jpg" alt="Deng Xiaoping" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Deng_Xiaoping_at_the_arrival_ceremony_for_the_Vice_Premier_of_China_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1978 the reforms of <b>Deng Xiaoping</b> restored family farming under the household responsibility system, opened Special Economic Zones such as Shenzhen in 1980 and gradually admitted private enterprise and foreign investment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Deng Xiaoping</b>’s saying that a cat is a good cat if it can catch rats, whether white or black, has been compared with the Han Feizi.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/legalism">Legalism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Deng Xiaoping</b> condemned “bourgeois liberalization” as a threat to the stability of the People’s Republic of China.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>In China, <b>Deng Xiaoping</b> held that Maoism showed the dangers of “ultra-leftism” and the harm done by the mass movements of its era, but he separated the revolutionary Mao from the ruler, judging him “70% right, 30% wrong”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote>Because of its association with the Cultural Revolution it was rarely heard after <b>Deng Xiaoping</b>'s rise in the late 1970s, and some in China regard it as an uncomfortable reminder of the cult of personality around Mao.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-east-is-red">The East Is Red</a></li><li><blockquote>Shelley Rigger writes that the leaders of the People's Republic tried to suppress her music while it spread; by the end of the 1970s it was said that <b>Deng Xiaoping</b> ruled by day and Teresa Teng by night.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-moon-represents-my-heart">The Moon Represents My Heart</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Xiexing Town, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.54,106.64&amp;year=1964" aria-label="Xiexing Town on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7314 1050 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Deng Xiaoping"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7764 1320h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7764 1320h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.54,106.64&amp;year=1964">What was here in 1964? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
